1
00:00:00,651 --> 00:00:08,096
(Q31) Scientists believe that a majority of the earth’s bird population migrate in some fashion or other.

2
00:00:08,096 --> 00:00:19,099
Some travel seasonally for relatively short distances, such as birds that move from their winter habitats in lowlands to mountain tops for the summers.

3
00:00:19,099 --> 00:00:29,542
Others, like the Arctic Tern, travel more than 25,000 miles seasonally between the northern and southern poles.

4
00:00:29,542 --> 00:00:36,365
Bird migration has been studied over many centuries through a variety of observations.

5
00:00:36,365 --> 00:00:43,667
But until relatively recently, where birds went to in the winter was considered something of a mystery.

6
00:00:43,667 --> 00:00:54,005
The lack of modern science and technology led to many theories that we now recognize as error-filled and even somewhat amusing.

7
00:00:54,005 --> 00:01:08,328
Take hibernation theory for example – two thousand years ago, it was commonly believed that when birds left an area, they went underwater to hibernate in the seas and oceans.

8
00:01:08,328 --> 00:01:20,695
[Q31答案句] Another theory for the regular appearance and disappearance of birds was that they spent winter hidden in mud till the weather changed and food became abundant again.

9
00:01:20,695 --> 00:01:34,319
(Q32) The theory that some birds hibernate persisted until experiments were done on caged birds in the 1940s which demonstrated that birds have no hibernation instinct.

10
00:01:34,319 --> 00:01:50,082
One of the earliest naturalists and philosophers from ancient Greece was Aristotle who was the first writer to discuss the disappearance and reappearance of some bird species at certain times of year.

11
00:01:50,082 --> 00:02:00,253
He developed the theory of transmutation, the seasonal change of one species into another, by observing redstarts and robins.

12
00:02:00,253 --> 00:02:16,117
[Q32答案句] He observed that in the autumn, small birds called ‘redstarts’ began to lose their feathers, which convinced Aristotle that they changed into robins for the winter, and back into redstarts in the summer.

13
00:02:16,117 --> 00:02:30,002
(Q33) [Q33答案句] These assumptions are understandable given that this pair of species are similar in shape, but are a classic example of an incorrect interpretation based on correct observations.

14
00:02:30,002 --> 00:02:39,171
(Q34) The most bizarre theory was put forward by an English amateur scientist, Charles Morton, in the seventeenth century.

15
00:02:39,171 --> 00:02:47,566
[Q34答案句] He wrote a surprisingly well-regarded paper claiming that birds migrate to the moon and back every year.

16
00:02:47,566 --> 00:02:59,159
He came to this conclusion as the only logical explanation for the total disappearance of some species.

17
00:02:59,159 --> 00:03:09,925
(Q35) One of the key moments in the development of migration theory came in 1822 when a white stork was shot in Germany.

18
00:03:09,925 --> 00:03:25,121
[Q35答案句] This particular stork made history because of the long spear in its neck which incredibly had not killed it – everyone immediately realised this spear was definitely not European.

19
00:03:25,121 --> 00:03:29,919
It turned out to be a spear from a tribe in Central Africa.

20
00:03:29,919 --> 00:03:41,741
(Q36) [Q36答案句] This was a truly defining moment in the history of ornithology because it was the first evidence that storks spend their winters in sub-Saharan Africa.

21
00:03:41,741 --> 00:03:50,979
(Q37) You can still see the ‘arrow stork’ in the Zoological Collection of the University of Rostock in Germany.

22
00:03:50,979 --> 00:04:06,368
People gradually became aware that European birds moved south in autumn and north in summer but didn’t know much about it until the practice of catching birds and putting rings on their legs became established.

23
00:04:06,368 --> 00:04:16,494
[Q37答案句] Before this, very little information was available about the actual destinations of particular species and how they travelled there.

24
00:04:16,494 --> 00:04:25,350
(Q38) People speculated that larger birds provided a kind of taxi service for smaller birds by carrying them on their backs.

25
00:04:25,350 --> 00:04:35,274
[Q38答案句] This idea came about because it seemed impossible that small birds weighing only a few grams could fly over vast oceans.

26
00:04:35,274 --> 00:04:45,387
(Q39) This idea was supported by observations of bird behaviour such as the harassment of larger birds by smaller birds.

27
00:04:45,387 --> 00:04:55,858
The development of bird ringing, by a Danish schoolteacher, Hans Christian Cornelius Mortensen, made many discoveries possible.

28
00:04:55,858 --> 00:05:09,396
[Q39答案句] This is still common practice today and relies upon what is known as ‘recovery’ – this is when ringed birds are found dead in the place they have migrated to, and identified.

29
00:05:09,396 --> 00:05:21,298
(Q40) Huge amounts of data were gathered in the early part of the twentieth century and for the first time in history people understood where birds actually went to in winter.

30
00:05:21,298 --> 00:05:30,093
[Q40答案句] In 1931, an atlas was published showing where the most common species of European birds migrated to.&nbsp;

31
00:05:30,093 --> 00:05:32,593
More recent theories about bird migration …

